can a retrovirus gave similar  results at youth as to what they had  been getting in their plastic base  system and there will be references for  both of these results at the end of the  talk and moving on to the allergen aook  model which is a case study in scaling  out the quantum system from one machine  to ten machines so this case study was a  collaboration between a thesis and its  European subsidiary Regenesis and the  contract manufacturing organization of  master cell so the object was to  transfer the process of so growing map  C's in the quantum to scale that process  out from one machine to ten machines to  create a larger lot size and then  finally to see if there are any  efficiencies that could be gained and  cutting straight to the chase we found  that there was a 40% cost a 40%  reduction in the cost of goods producing  cells in this way so in the first slide  we show the technology transfer a time  line  through to scale out so the first few  weeks were spent simply learning how to  run the quantum and then another six  weeks were used for process optimization  most of this work was on downstream  processing of the cell product and  finally the ten quantum runs were done  in the scale out so this is a comparison  of running a single quantum system here  in the box on the bottom it tells you  how many people are required so you can  see on day one we take two operators to  run the quantum system that's mostly to  prepare the cells and the quantum for  loading with the ten quantum system you  see at the box at the top that two  operators are also required in day zero  and day one or day minus one and day  zero but basically the intermediate  steps can be handled by a single  operator and as I said before the  harvest day is the most difficult and  here we needed more help so finally if  you look at the product that we got you  can see that the flow cytometry profiles  from the comparing the quantum produced  cells to the self stack produce cells  are equal and same the same is true for  the protein secretion so you can see  that we were able to develop a process  that can yield consistent cell qualify  quality and yield and can simplify the  progression from learning to production  and importantly there's a 40% cost  reduction in cost of goods and we were  able to produce large lots of cells so  here are the references for the  lentiviral the retroviral and finally  the scale out of the quantum cell  expansion system thank you very much  Thank You dr. Zeltzer for that  informative presentation it is time for  Q&amp;A if you have a question you'd like to  ask dr. belt sir please do so now just  click on the green Q&amp;A button at the  lower left of the presentation window  type your question into the box it  appears on your screen and click on the  send button well answer as many of your  questions as we have time for let's get  started our first question is can you  control the shear stress in the quantum  for the growth of endothelial cells  thanks for that question first of all  there's very little cheer stress in the  quantum system that's pure physics if  you've got a quarter inch Inlet line  going into the icy loop and a two inch  in diameter bioreactor there's very  little shear that happens having said  that though we can control the flow rate  of the media through the icy loop with  our pumps and if needed we can even feed  across the membrane without creating  shear stress how can I monitor the  growth of the cells in the quantum so  you can monitor the growth in the in the  quantum by taking a sample from the EEC  sample port so that's a lower lock port  that is sterile so there's a point two  micron filter behind it we recommend  that you monitor the lactate production  and glucose consumption and typically  those measurements are done once every  24 hours has the quant have been used to  culture IPS cells yes the quantum has  been used to successfully expand IPS  cells we haven't done differentiation of  IPS cells yet in the quantum but that's  ongoing in fact a recent experiments  have shown that the quantum can  significantly reduce the media required  to expand IPS cells what is the media  perfusion rate in the quantum well the  media perfusion rate typically starts  out at zero or very low so it's at one  point one mil per minute  in the beginning stages of a typical  expansion and it may go as high as one  point six mils per minute at the end of  the typical MSC expansion for viral  vectors and and other applications we  may have to go higher as the cell  densities increase within the quantum so  for the viral vector case we are running  up around 2.5 million it is there a  reason for switching to serum free media  for viral vector production ah yes so  that's a good question if you produce  your viral vectors in serum containing  media it complicates the concentration  of that virus product after you have  finished your run so the serum proteins  would quickly clog the membrane  concentrators that are typically used to  concentrate viral vectors how are the  dynabeads loaded into the system for T  cell expansions very simple we basically  mix the dynabeads with the cells in a  cell inlet bag prior prior to loading  the self into the bioreactor and as I  mentioned yeah the ratio is 2 to 1 what  kind of media is used for T cell  expansions today we tried both Tex Max  and rpm I plus 2% human a B serum and  right now we get the best results with  our PMI but we plan to test more media  in the future so stay tuned we're almost  out of time so this will have to be our  last question how our suspension cells  kept in the hollow fiber bioreactor so  our pumps the circulation pumps in the  quantum expect cell expansion system can  be reversed so we can run those pumps in  such a fashion as to push any cell that  may have gotten out into the rest of the  IC loop back into the bioreactor so  again we just use our circulation pumps  I would like to once again thank  Beltzer for his presentation do you have  any final comments I just want to thank  everybody and if you have any questions  further to this please contact me at my  email that should be on the last slide  of the presentation well thank you once  again I would also like to thank our  sponsor to room o BCT for making today's  educational webcast possible this  webinar has been approved for continuing  educational credits please click on the  CDE button at the bottom left corner and  follow the process to receive your  credits and before we go I want to let  everyone know that today's webcast will  be available for on-demand viewing  through November 31st 2017 you'll  receive an email from lab roots letting  you know when this webcast will be  available for replay please share that  announcement with your colleagues who  may have missed today's live event  that's all for now thank you for joining  us and we hope to see you again soon  goodbye
 

